      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "The best overall roofing material for Utah is standing seam metal for long-term mountain durability, and architectural asphalt shingles\n\nfor standard budget-friendly valley needs. Utah weather demands materials that handle heavy winter snow loads, freeze-thaw cycles, high winds, and intense summer UV rays.",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "standing seam metal"
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          0,
          1,
          2,
          3
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Metal Roofing (Best for Mountain & High-Snow Areas)"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Performance: Excellent; smooth panels shed heavy snow instantly and prevent ice dam formation."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Lifespan:\n40 to 70+ years."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Utah Advantage: Resists high winds (140+ mph), handles rapid temperature swings without cracking, and reflects solar heat in the summer. Ideal for high-elevation areas like Park City or Heber Valley."
          }
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          4,
          5
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Architectural Asphalt Shingles (Best Value Standard)"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Performance: Good; reliable baseline protection when upgraded to Class 4 impact resistance."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Lifespan:\n20 to 30 years."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Utah Advantage: Most affordable option and handles standard valley weather (Salt Lake, Provo) well, though extreme UV and temperature shifts can cause premature aging. Look for high-wind ratings (110\u2013130 mph)."
          }
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          6,
          7
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Concrete and Clay Tile (Best for Desert Aesthetic)"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Performance: Excellent; heavy mass keeps tiles locked down in wind."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Lifespan:\n50 to 100+ years."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Utah Advantage: Concrete tiles stand up well to intense sun and moderate snow, though concrete handles northern Utah freeze-thaw cycles better than fragile clay. Requires a reinforced roof structure due to high weight."
          }
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          8,
          9,
          10
        ]
      },
